-- title:
--   The set densely generated by a gate set
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal H=\mathbb C^{\iota}$ be a finite-dimensional Hilbert space and let $G$ be a set of unitary operators on $\mathcal H$ (a **gate set**). The set **densely generated** by $G$ is
--   $$
--   \overline{\langle G\rangle}=\overline{\{\,g_1g_2\cdots g_m \;:\; m\ge 0,\ g_1,\dots,g_m\in G\,\}},
--   $$
--   the topological closure of the set of all finite products of gates (the empty product being the identity $I$). A gate set $G$ *densely generates* a set $S$ of operators when $S\subseteq\overline{\langle G\rangle}$: every element of $S$ can be approximated to every accuracy $\epsilon>0$ by a sequence of gates from $G$.
--
--   This is the notion behind the book's universal gate sets (Definition 4.3.1) and the phrase "densely generate" in Lemmas 4.3.1 and 4.3.2; every statement of the mission is phrased through it.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Operators are matrices `Matrix ι ι ℂ` with the entrywise topology (on a finite-dimensional space, the operator-norm topology); gates are elements of `Matrix.unitaryGroup ι ℂ`. Products are taken in the submonoid generated by $G$; since the unitary group is compact, its closure coincides with the closure of the subgroup generated by $G$.
-- source:
--   Bacon, Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, Definition 4.3.1, p.50 (PDF p.67); "densely generate", §4.3.1, p.52 (PDF p.69)

import Mathlib

namespace BaconThesis.Universality

/-- **The set densely generated by a gate set.**
Bacon, *Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers*, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1,
§4.3, Definition 4.3.1, p.50 (PDF p.67), and the phrase "densely generate" of §4.3.1, p.52
(PDF p.69): a gate set can be used to approximate a unitary to every accuracy `ε > 0` by a
sequence (product) of its gates.

For a set `G` of unitary matrices on `ℂ^ι`, `denseGen G` is the topological closure, inside
`Matrix ι ι ℂ`, of the set of all finite products of elements of `G` (the empty product being the
identity). A gate set `G` *densely generates* a set `S` of matrices when `S ⊆ denseGen G`.

**Formalization Note.** Hilbert spaces are `ι → ℂ` with `Fintype ι`, operators are
`Matrix ι ι ℂ` with the entrywise (product) topology, which on a finite-dimensional space is the
operator-norm topology. Gates are elements of `Matrix.unitaryGroup ι ℂ`. Products of gates are
taken in the submonoid generated by `G` (sequences of gates, as in Definition 4.3.1); since the
unitary group is compact, the closure of this submonoid equals the closure of the subgroup
generated by `G`. -/
def denseGen {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι]
    (G : Set (Matrix.unitaryGroup ι ℂ)) : Set (Matrix ι ι ℂ) :=
  closure (Subtype.val '' (Submonoid.closure G : Set (Matrix.unitaryGroup ι ℂ)))

end BaconThesis.Universality


